Here is Geoffrey Chaucer’s poem **"Truth"** (also known as *Balade de Bon Conseyl*) in its original Middle English text:

Flee fro the prees, and dwelle with sothfastnesse,
Suffise unto thy good, though it be smal;
For hord hath hate, and clymbyng tikelnesse,
Prees hath envye, and wele blent overal;
Savour no more than thee bihove shal;
Reule wel thyself, that other folk canst rede;
And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.

Tempest thee noght al croked to amende,
In trust of hire that turneth as a bal;
Gret reste stant in litel besynesse;
Bewar also to spurne agayn an al;
Stryve not, as doth the crokke with the wal.
Daunte thyself, that dauntest otheres dede;
And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.

That thee is sent, receyve in buxumnesse;
The wrastling for this world axeth a fal.
Her is non hoome, her nys but wildernesse:
Forth, pilgrim, forth! Forth, beste, out of thy stal!
Know thy contree, look up, thank God of al;
Hold the hye wey, and lat thy gost thee lede;
And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.

**Envoy**

Therfore, thou Vache, leve thyn old wrecchednesse
Unto the world; leve fro being thral;
Crye him mercy, that of his high goodnesse
Made thee of noght, and in especial
Draw unto him, and pray in general
For thee and eek for other, hevenlich mede;
And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.

The poem you are looking for is **"Truth"** (also known as *Balade de Bon Conseyl* or the *Balade of Good Counsel*), written in the late 14th century by **Geoffrey Chaucer**. 

It is addressed directly to Chaucer's friend and contemporary, **Sir Philip de la Vache**, a courtier who had fallen out of political favor. Chaucer uses a clever pun on Vache's name (which means "cow" in French, echoed in the line *"Forth, beste, out of thy stal!"*) while offering him moral guidance to reject worldly ambition, accept fortune, and trust in divine truth.

Here is the original Middle English text, followed by a brief summary of its meaning:

---

### **Truth (Balade de Bon Conseyl)**
*by Geoffrey Chaucer*

Flee fro the prees, and dwelle with sothfastnesse,  
Suffyce unto thy good, though it be smal;  
For hord hath hate, and clymbyng tikelnesse,  
Prees hath envye, and wele blent overal;  
Savor no more than thee bihove shal;  
Reule wel thyself, that other folk canst rede;  
And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.  

Tempest thee noght al croked to redresse,  
In trust of hire that turneth as a bal;  
Gret reste stant in litel besynesse;  
An eek be war to spurne agayn an al;  
Stryve not, as doth the crokke with the wal.  
Daunte thyself, that dauntest otheres dede;  
And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.  

That thee is sent, receyve in buxumnesse;  
The wrastling for this world axeth a fal.  
Her is non hom, her nys but wilderness:  
Forth, pilgrim, forth! Forth, beste, out of thy stal!  
Know thy contree, look up, thank God of al;  
Hold the hye wey, and lat thy gost thee lede;  
And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.  

**Envoy**  
Therfore, thou **Vache**, leve thy old wrecchednesse;  
Unto the world leve of to be thral;  
Crye hym mercy, that in his high goodnesse  
Made thee of noght, and in especial  
Draw unto hym, and pray in general  
For thee and eek for other, hevenlich mede;  
And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.

---

### **Key Vocabulary & Modern Translation Guide**

* **Flee fro the prees:** Flee from the crowd/courtly turmoil.
* **Sothfastnesse:** Truthfulness / reality.
* **Clymbyng tikelnesse:** Social climbing is precarious/uncertain.
* **Wele blent overal:** Wealth/prosperity blinds everyone.
* **Hire that turneth as a bal:** Lady Fortune (and her spinning wheel).
* **Buxumnesse:** Humility / submission / grace.
* **The wrastling for this world axeth a fal:** Striving for worldly success guarantees a fall.
* **Envoy to Vache:** *"Therefore, Vache, leave your old wretchedness; stop being a thrall (slave) to the world. Ask mercy of Him who made you from nothing... and truth shall deliver you, there is no doubt."*

The poem you are referring to is **"Truth"** (also known as ***Balade de Bon Conseyl*** or by its opening line, ***"Flee fro the prees"***) by Geoffrey Chaucer. 

Here is the Middle English text (based on standard manuscript editions such as *The Riverside Chaucer*):

***

### **Truth: Balade de Bon Conseyl**

**I.**
Flee fro the prees, and dwelle with sothfastnesse,  
Suffyce unto thy good, though it be smal;  
For hord hath hate, and clymbyng tikelnesse,  
Prees hath envye, and wele blent overal;  
Savor no more than thee bihove shal;  
Reule wel thyself, that other folk canst rede;  
**And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.**  

**II.**
Tempest thee noght al croked to redresse,  
In trust of hire that turneth as a bal;  
Gret reste stant in litel besynesse;  
An eek be war to spurne agayn an al;  
Stryve not, as doth the crokke with the wal.  
Daunte thyself, that dauntest otheres dede;  
**And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.**  

**III.**
That thee is sent, receyve in buxumnesse;  
The wrastlyng for this world axeth a fal.  
Her is non hoom, her nis but wildernesse:  
Forth, pilgrim, forth! Forth, beste, out of thy stal!  
Know thy contree, look up, thank God of al;  
Hold the hye wey, and lat thy gost thee lede;  
**And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.**  

---

#### **Envoy** *(found in some manuscripts, addressed to Sir Philip de la Vache)*

Therfore, thou Vache, leve thyn old wrecchednesse  
Unto the world; leve off thy besynesse;  
Mynd answere to the lust that thee doth lede;  
**And trouthe thee shal delivere, it is no drede.**

***

### Notes on Spelling and Variations:
* **"It is no drede"** is a common Middle English idiom meaning "there is no doubt" or "it is certain."
* Depending on the specific medieval manuscript (such as MS Fairfax 16 or MS Cotton Otho A.xVIII), spelling varies slightly (e.g., *trouthe* / *trothe*, *shal* / *schal*, *delyvere* / *delivere*).
